WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.710
One last visit to the theatre.

2
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.201
A touring show uses the same prompt book every night.

3
00:00:08.201 --> 00:00:11.693
Say the stage manager wants every lighting cue timed.

4
00:00:11.693 --> 00:00:16.371
Nobody rewrites the script, and nobody hires a stand-in for each actor.

5
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:22.798
Instead, the stage manager slips a note into the prompt book before rehearsal: start a stopwatch here.

6
00:00:22.798 --> 00:00:28.387
And in an emergency, they can walk in mid-performance and paste a correction over one page.

7
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.907
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:36.842
Can you really change the code of a program that's already running, without a restart?

9
00:00:36.842 --> 00:00:39.655
And if you can, can you change anything you like?

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:43.913
Lessons 4 and 5 added work by wrapping objects.

11
00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:47.499
That needs an interface, or a class that isn't final.

12
00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:50.544
It misses self calls when the proxy forwards.

13
00:00:50.544 --> 00:00:54.265
And it only works on objects created through the proxy.

14
00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:56.768
Timing a method inside a library jar?

15
00:00:56.768 --> 00:00:57.648
Out of reach.

16
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:02.295
A Java agent works on the class instead of the object.

17
00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.779
It's an ordinary class with a special entry point.

18
00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:11.910
The JVM hands it an Instrumentation, the JDK's handle for watching and changing classes.

19
00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.069
That's the stage manager's key.

20
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.470
The simplest way in is a flag: java -javaagent, then the agent's jar and its arguments.

21
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.263
The JVM calls the agent's premain before your main.

22
00:01:25.263 --> 00:01:31.511
The jar's manifest, a small text file inside it, names the class with Premain-Class.

23
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.016
The first thing most agents do is register a ClassFileTransformer.

24
00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:46.599
From then on, the JVM calls its transform method with the bytes of every class it is about to define: the class files of the last lesson.

25
00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:50.795
transform returns new bytes, or null, which means no change.

26
00:01:50.795 --> 00:01:56.811
The demo's agent only watches: it prints each class's name and size, and returns null.

27
00:01:57.733 --> 00:02:02.843
The demo starts a second JVM with this agent, and the argument opening-night.

28
00:02:02.843 --> 00:02:06.758
The program prints main started, then uses a Ticket record.

29
00:02:06.758 --> 00:02:09.479
When does the agent see the Ticket class?

30
00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.404
Before main starts, or later?

31
00:02:11.404 --> 00:02:12.465
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.693
Here's what it printed on JDK 25.

33
00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:23.509
premain ran first, when 890 JDK classes had already loaded.

34
00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:27.753
Then the agent saw AgentTarget, before main started.

35
00:02:27.753 --> 00:02:29.549
And the Ticket record?

36
00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:31.671
Just before its first use.

37
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:36.839
So 890 classes were in their seats before the agent even said hello.

38
00:02:36.839 --> 00:02:39.691
The JDK always gets to rehearsal early.

39
00:02:39.691 --> 00:02:48.100
But nearly every class of your own program loads after premain returns, so a startup agent sees almost all of them.

40
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:52.859
That's lesson 1's props, fetched only when a scene first needs them.

41
00:02:52.859 --> 00:02:57.853
And no warning appeared: an agent named on the command line is on the guest list.

42
00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:03.629
The rule: a transformer sees each class once, as the JVM defines it.

43
00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:07.301
Register it before the classes you care about load.

44
00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:10.225
But what about a class that loaded an hour ago?

45
00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:13.733
For that, there's retransformClasses.

46
00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:20.931
It runs the registered transformers again on classes that are already loaded, and installs the result in place.

47
00:03:20.931 --> 00:03:25.860
The Class object stays the same, so every existing object sees the new code.

48
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:31.804
The second demo has a final class, StageClock, whose now returns tick.

49
00:03:31.804 --> 00:03:33.181
It creates a clock.

50
00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:38.252
Then it attaches an agent to its own JVM, and adds timing code to now.

51
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.552
The clock was created before the agent existed.

52
00:03:41.552 --> 00:03:45.710
On its next call, which body runs, the old one or the new one?

53
00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:46.783
Pause and guess.

54
00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:51.597
The new one.

55
00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:52.966
Before: tick.

56
00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:59.388
Then, from inside now: StageClock.now took 14176 nanoseconds.

57
00:03:59.388 --> 00:04:00.652
After: tick.

58
00:04:00.652 --> 00:04:04.127
The same object, with no restart.

59
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:05.447
It still says tick.

60
00:04:05.447 --> 00:04:08.181
It just has someone holding a stopwatch now.

61
00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:12.841
And its class is final, so no subclass from lesson 5 could have touched it.

62
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:16.558
The JDK's documentation adds three things.

63
00:04:16.558 --> 00:04:22.141
A call that's already running finishes on the old code; new calls get the new code.

64
00:04:22.141 --> 00:04:30.078
The initializer doesn't run again, so static fields keep their values: lesson 1's dress rehearsal is never held twice.

65
00:04:30.078 --> 00:04:33.643
And if retransformation throws, no class was changed.

66
00:04:34.533 --> 00:04:36.323
There's also a hard limit.

67
00:04:36.323 --> 00:04:44.651
Retransformation must not add, remove or rename fields or methods, change signatures or modifiers, or change inheritance.

68
00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:46.854
You can rewrite what a cue says.

69
00:04:46.854 --> 00:04:49.813
You can't add a character to the cast list.

70
00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:53.360
And here the prompt book picture breaks.

71
00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.168
A correction pasted into one prompt book changes one copy.

72
00:04:57.168 --> 00:04:59.598
A class exists once per class loader.

73
00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:06.099
So one retransformation changes the code for every object of that class, and every caller, at once.

74
00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:11.070
Retransformation swaps method bodies in place, for every caller at once.

75
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.405
It never changes the class's shape.

76
00:05:14.333 --> 00:05:15.444
One more thing.

77
00:05:15.444 --> 00:05:21.143
That demo also printed four lines of warning, because its agent arrived late.

78
00:05:21.143 --> 00:05:27.286
Since JDK 21, the JVM prints a warning for each agent loaded into it while it runs.

79
00:05:27.286 --> 00:05:33.133
The flag EnableDynamicAgentLoading, with a plus, opts in and hides the warning.

80
00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.030
So, the names.

81
00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:39.643
The stage manager is the agent, and the key is the Instrumentation.

82
00:05:39.643 --> 00:05:43.980
The note slipped in before rehearsal is a ClassFileTransformer.

83
00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.491
The correction pasted mid-show is retransformation.

84
00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:49.444
Rules of thumb.

85
00:05:49.444 --> 00:05:53.619
To see a class as it loads, register early, with -javaagent.

86
00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:58.281
Change bodies, never the shape: a count must live in another class.

87
00:05:58.281 --> 00:06:03.360
And if an agent arrives late, expect the warning, and decide on the flag.

88
00:06:04.267 --> 00:06:06.881
So, can you change a running program?

89
00:06:06.881 --> 00:06:12.108
Its method bodies, yes, with no restart, under objects that already exist.

90
00:06:12.108 --> 00:06:13.097
Its shape, no.

91
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:14.932
Quick check.

92
00:06:14.932 --> 00:06:21.224
To count calls to StageClock.now, your retransformation adds a field, long calls.

93
00:06:21.224 --> 00:06:22.233
What happens?

94
00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:26.087
It can't.

95
00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:28.638
Retransformation must not add fields.

96
00:06:28.638 --> 00:06:34.361
The count must live somewhere else, such as a public class that the new code calls.
